# JARVIS boot config .gitignore
#
# Written 2026-08-02, before this tree was ever a git repository.
#
# This tree is small and is almost entirely the point of the repo: CLAUDE.md
# (the boot config) and .claude/agents/*.md (the team). Those are tracked.

# ─── LOCAL CLAUDE CODE SETTINGS ───────────────────────────────────────────
# .claude/settings.local.json is excluded on the strength of Anthropic's own
# documentation, checked 2026-08-02 at https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings
# which states it is "for settings that are not checked in" and that Claude
# Code adds "**/.claude/settings.local.json" to the global git excludes file
# when it writes one — and that if the file is created by hand, "add it to
# your gitignore yourself". This is that.
#
# Verified separately on this machine: the current file contains no
# secret-shaped value (zero runs of 30+ token characters). It is excluded on
# convention and on the forward risk — that file is where credentials for MCP
# servers and hooks would land later — not because it is dirty today.
#
# Consequence Mark should know about: the permission allowlist will not be
# version controlled, so there is no rollback on it. If he would rather have
# that history than the guard, delete these three lines.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/*.local.json
**/.claude/settings.local.json

# ─── CLAUDE CODE RUNTIME ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
.claude/todos/
.claude/shell-snapshots/
.claude/statsig/
.claude/projects/
.claude/history.jsonl

# ─── THE REQUEST RECORD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Held out of version control for the same reason as the three agent files at
# the bottom of this file, arriving from a different direction.
#
# These two hold Mark's asks in his own words: .requests-inbox.md is the raw
# hook capture of every prompt he sends, REQUESTS.md is the written-up version.
# Sooner or later one of them says something about the private matter — "chase
# the discovery deadline", a name, a date — and a commit is permanent. There is
# no remote on this repo today, but the warning at the bottom of this file
# applies here too: if one is ever added, this history goes with it.
#
# Losing history on these costs little. They are a working record, not code,
# and the machine's own backup covers the disk. The tooling that writes them —
# hooks/log-request.py and hooks/boot-requests.py — IS tracked, because the
# mechanism is worth versioning and carries none of Mark's words.
#
# THAT LAST SENTENCE WAS FALSE FROM THE DAY IT WAS WRITTEN, AND IT IS TRUE NOW
# ONLY BECAUSE 2026-08-04 CAUGHT IT. Neither hook was ever `git add`ed. The whole
# of hooks/ sat untracked — `git status` said `?? hooks/` and `git check-ignore`
# said nothing at all — so `git clean -fd` in this tree would have deleted the
# entire request-persistence mechanism, on a box with one disk and no remote,
# while this comment sat here telling the next session the files were safe.
#
# That is the specific failure worth naming: not an untracked file, but a
# reassurance in a tracked file that stopped anybody looking. A comment that
# asserts a state is a claim, and a claim that nothing verifies rots silently in
# the direction that makes you comfortable. Corrected by adding the files, not by
# softening the sentence.
REQUESTS.md
.requests-inbox.md

# AND ITS BACKUPS, which is not a detail. Added 2026-08-07: before handing
# REQUESTS.md to a specialist to edit in place, a copy is taken beside it. That
# copy is a byte-for-byte duplicate of the file two lines above — Mark's words,
# verbatim, private matter and all — and it lands UNTRACKED rather than ignored,
# so the very next `git add -A` sweeps it into a permanent commit while the
# original is correctly held back. The protection above is worth exactly nothing
# if the copy walks around it.
.REQUESTS-backup-*.md

# AND THE 2026-09-11 COMPACTION ARCHIVES — same reason as the two lines above,
# arriving from a different direction. memory-tools/compact_requests.py and
# compact_inbox.py split the closed/synced-old content OUT of the two files
# above into these; they are literally excerpts of the same text, so the same
# "Mark's words, private matter and all" warning applies to them unchanged.
# (*.bak-* below already covers the REQUESTS.md.bak-<ts> / .requests-inbox.md.bak-<ts>
# backups those scripts also write.)
REQUESTS-archive-2026-09-11.md
.requests-inbox-archive-2026-09-11.md

# ─── memory-tools (semantic index over the memory above) ─────────────────
# index.db is a generated, rebuildable SQLite file (`python3 indexer.py
# --rebuild` regenerates it from source in under a minute). It is also, by
# construction, a searchable copy of most of this repo's own memory content —
# committing a binary blob of that is both pointless (it's derived) and the
# wrong shape of thing to keep in git history. exclude.py's denylist keeps the
# private matter out of it, but "keep it out of the index" and "keep it out of
# git" are two different disciplines and this repo doesn't need to rely on only
# the first one.
memory-tools/index.db
memory-tools/__pycache__/

# ─── SECRETS (guards) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.env
*.env.*
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
.netrc

# ─── HAND-ROLLED BACKUPS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Guard. In voice-line a *.bak copy of an env file still held a live token.
# Backups are snapshots nobody audited; they are ignored on sight.
*.bak
*.bak.*
*.backup
*.orig
*.rej
*.old
*.pre-*

# ─── JUNK ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
node_modules/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# Directory rule was missing; only *.log was present. logs/vault-versions/*.md would have been tracked.
logs/
.claude/settings.local.json

# agentic_os/'s chat-capture cursor: which line of each session transcript
# has already been written into the vault. Machine state, not source, and it
# is specific to this box's own transcript history -- committing it would
# make a fresh checkout claim to have already captured chat it never saw.
agentic_os/.state/

# HELD OUT OF VERSION CONTROL, DELIBERATELY. Do not remove these three lines.
#
# The comment that used to sit here explained why, in terms that themselves
# disclosed what the files disclose - in a tracked, committed file. That is the
# same defect it was warning about, one layer out: a rule written so carefully
# that the writing leaks. Found 2026-08-03 by the specialist whose job is
# exactly this, reviewing his own configuration.
#
# The old text stays in git history and cannot be removed without rewriting it.
# There is no remote on this repo, so today that history is on one disk and
# nowhere else. IF A REMOTE IS EVER ADDED, THIS HISTORY GOES WITH IT - deal with
# that before the first push, not after.
#
# These three carry no case facts. They are held out because a commit is
# permanent and their existence is the disclosure. If they are ever to be
# versioned, that is Mark's decision and it is genuinely irreversible.
.claude/agents/sable.md
.claude/agents/redmond.md
.claude/agents/holloway.md

# IN-FLIGHT.md holds Mark's verbatim words and the state of work in progress,
# and it has already carried a line acknowledging the private matter exists.
# Same reasoning as REQUESTS.md above: never committed. Added 2026-08-14.
IN-FLIGHT.md
IN-FLIGHT-*.md

# BULK AND DERIVED — added 2026-08-15, when a checkpoint commit was asked for
# and `git status` had 36 untracked entries hiding two enormous ones. These are
# not secrets and not decisions; they are weight. `lan-serve/` alone is 1.5 GB
# across 1905 files and `openjarvis-base/` is 99 MB across 1949 — committing
# either into a repo with NO REMOTE buys nothing and makes every future clone,
# diff and status slower forever. A repo that is painful to read is one nobody
# checks, which is how 19 changed files sat uncommitted for a day.
lan-serve/
openjarvis-base/
.wrangler/
backups/
alpaca-removed-*/

# Timestamped hand-backups. The house makes these constantly before risky edits
# (`*.bak-before-*`, `*.bak-20260811-*`). Git is the thing that makes them
# unnecessary; committing them puts the crutch in the record beside the fix.
*.bak
*.bak-*
.jobs-backup-*

# Raw pastes and drafted outbound messages hold Mark's verbatim words and
# client-facing drafts, same reasoning as .requests-inbox.md — never committed.
inbox-paste/
outbox/
# Large render captures; regenerable, not source.
board-catalog/

# The natural voices, staged into the Windows installer by ship-windows.sh.
# 115 MB of model weights that are fetched once and never edited — they are a
# build input, not source, and a repository is the wrong place to keep them.
desktop/installer/tts/
desktop/installer/node/

# ─── GENERATED ARTEFACTS, added 2026-08-29 ────────────────────────────────
# 197 MB of untracked material had accumulated and drift-check.py was
# correctly complaining every boot. Almost all of it is OUTPUT: screenshots
# a render produced, Word documents a script builds, media pulled from an
# API. Committing it would put 190 MB of regenerable bytes in front of every
# future clone, and worse, it would bury the source that actually matters.
#
# The rule: if a script in this repo can rebuild it, it is not tracked. The
# script is.
desktop/shots/
desktop/heygen/
desktop/site/assets/
*.docx
*.xlsx
*.mp4
*.wav
render-check/

# Spent hook backups. Git history keeps them; a loose .bak beside a live
# hook reads as work somebody still owes.
hooks/*.bak-*
helloim-social-skills/
social-media-skills/
helloim-social-skills/**
social-media-skills/**

helloim-app/Windows/windows-source/src-tauri/target/
